Z||000016||blkcofy||07-10-2001||10:27 PM||||Skitime<BR>First of all, you are a fiero master!  I started searching the net for the kit you used for your fiero, and here to find out you machined it yourself!  Awesome, simply awesome.  The magic you've created is the fact that everything you've done, looks wonderfully stock.  I'm jealous and proud, all at the same time! Ever thought about doing some similar work for some of us less talented?  If your seriously interested, so am I!!  Name the logistics and cost, and I'm your customer!!  I'm reconditioning my internals, but I'd love to start getting the exterior work started too!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000017||blkcofy||07-10-2001||10:29 PM||||Skitime<BR>First of all, you are a fiero master!  I started searching the net for the kit you used for your fiero, and here to find out you machined it yourself!  Awesome, simply awesome.  The magic you've created is the fact that everything you've done, looks wonderfully stock.  I'm jealous and proud, all at the same time! Ever thought about doing some similar work for some of us less talented?  If your seriously interested, so am I!!  Name the logistics and cost, and I'm your customer!!  I'm reconditioning my internals, but I'd love to start getting the exterior work started too!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000018||skitime||07-11-2001||10:12 AM||||Gosh, Blkcofy you almost make me blush. The praise is much appreciated.  I would love to do work for others but I don't see how time will allow it. So I just share it here. So much time goes into the planning/developing of ideas.  What I am saying I guess is there isn't a clock for me to work on my car but I don't know if it would be worthwhile to work on someone elses.  I went the opposite way of doing the exterior first although more changes are coming but will probably do an engine swap this winter. I will email you some hi resolution pictures of the car.  BTW, welcome to the forum.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Hartz||07-11-2001||10:43 AM||||Verrry nice car.<P>Question - you said you painted all of the body panels off the car?  Why did you decide to go this route?  Did you change the color of the car?  My '84 needs paint badly, and I have heard mixed opinions on painting the panels on/off the car.  I can't argue with your results.  If only mine turns out half as nice!<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||14
Z||000020||skitime||07-11-2001||11:56 AM||||Thank you Hartz. No I painted it the original color.  If you ask yourself how was the car painted originally and why did Pontiac do it that way then you know why I did too.  If you want all the edges perfect with no overspray the panels must come off.  The other advantage is the painter can have every panel in the optimum position for spraying. My painter informed me that I had 43 individual pieces that were painted. How many rocker panels on Fieros are painted perfectly on the bottom?  Another advantage is you are less afraid to continue modifying the car. I painted the modified trunk at a later date. With this car, I kept all the original panels and had them painted too even if they were not going on the car that way I could always return it to original. I am getting quite a collection of newly painted parts setting around though such as quarter panels, trunk, sail panels, trim, mirrors etc.. There are risks in painting it this way.  My advise if you do an off panel paint job is realize that you will need more paint.  Order three times more paint than normal and have it mixed all at once. Plan you polishing the paint afterwards (I haven't done this yet) so the finished surface will match too.||||reg||1
